# Shell scripts must stay LF so they run on the Linux CI runners (a CRLF carriage
# return would break `#!/usr/bin/env bash` and every command). The Wave 3b
# isolation-proof job (`engine/ci/isolation_proof.sh`) depends on this.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The git hook in tools/githooks has no extension, so the *.sh rule above does not
# reach it. It is run by git's own shell; a carriage return would break its shebang
# and every line of it, and the canonical update law would silently stop running
# locally.
tools/githooks/* text eol=lf
# The README's figures must stay LF in the WORKING TREE on every platform, because
# `tools/check_artwork_geometry.py` reads the bytes it finds there and one of its rules is that
# these files are LF. MEASURED: without this line the gate is green on a box whose
# `core.autocrlf` is `input` and RED on `windows-latest`, whose default is `true` -- the runner's
# checkout converts every sheet and the gate then reports fourteen files for a property of git's
# configuration rather than of the commit. The committed bytes were already LF, so this line
# changes no content; it makes the property the gate checks true wherever the tree is checked out.
*.svg text eol=lf
